A||||9||Tom88gt||oil change at 3000 or 7500?||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||12-04-2000||06:12 PM||||My owner's manual says oil change at 3000 miles.  But Haynes says oil change at 7500 miles.  Which to go with?  Yes, I know that there is no harm changing every 3000 miles, or even sooner.  But is there any real benefit to it?<P>My 1992 Jeep Wrangler is 7500 miles in both the owners manual and Haynes.  My 1975 914 Porsche is 3000 in Haynes.  My 1999 Harley is 5000 miles (which I find to be very surprising as air cooled engines are very hard on their oil).<P>So, obviously the 3000 rule is not written in stone.  I know all the oil companies and oil change shops recommend 3000.  But they make more money if you change oil more often than required.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||12-04-2000||06:46 PM||||I've been changing the oil and filter on my 84 every 5,000 miles since the car was new. The engine now has 231,000 miles on it.  When I rebuilt it about 60,000 miles ago, there was no sludge build up at all internally.  Also, all the internal parts measured within new tolerances except for the cylinders, which were borderline.  5,000 mile intervals are easy to keep up with and fall between the "normal" and "severe" intervals in the owners manual.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||12-04-2000||07:10 PM||||Changing the oil/filter at 3000 mi is cheap insurance. Have you looked at the cost of a rebuild these days. I also run a qt of Marvel Mystery Oil with every oil change. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||12-04-2000||08:31 PM||||I run Mobil1 5W-30 in all of my cars with a 5,000 change interval (both oil and filter).  <P>This has worked very for me for many years.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-04-2000||08:58 PM||||Depends on the type of driving you do. Mostly stop and go city driving, heavy towing, desert heat, or lots of dusty gravel roads... you'd better change more often. I drive mostly highway miles so I change at every 5,000 miles (nice and easy to remember). I have a Suburban with 272,000 miles and it's still hanging in there...the oil was never changed sooner than every 5,000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||12-04-2000||09:21 PM||||uhhh every 500-750 unless on a road trip then it is at each ocean and once in between.||||reg||1
Z||000006||PlasticWrapd||12-05-2000||10:58 AM||||I have some info that may help with this "age old" question.  While working for a trucking company doing routine maintenence, I had to take samples of motor oil to a local lab for testing.  At the time I owned a new Subaru Legacy with about 15K miles on it.  I had the oil tested after about 3500 miles on two different occasions.  I got the results, but was unsure what they meant.  I went back to the lab and asked the tech.  He explained the different levels of contaminents in the oil to me and what it meant.  To make a long story short, the oil had enough contamination to allow premature wear.  This was regular mineral based oil.  The trucks that I had tested, used synthetic.  Now these engines were diesel, but the oil was still offering adequate protection even after 15K miles of use!  So the choice is yours.  To me, the 3K oil change is some real cheap insurance!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||12-05-2000||06:29 PM||||I agree, every 3,000 miles is cheap insurance. I try to change it every 3K, but somtimes I fall behind. I would say 3K to 4K miles.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rrazzz||12-06-2000||06:02 PM||||3,000 to 5,000 miles is about right for average conditions and average distance per month. If you don't drive 1,000 per month, then change by the number of months not the number of miles. Most maintenance recommendations are based on xK miles = x months. If you want to be safe and change oil at 3,000 miles, then change at 3 months if you only drive a few short trips. Even if you are going to wait for "5,000 miles," do not wait more than 5 months before changing oil.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DougV||12-06-2000||06:43 PM||||I change mine every month(about 2500-3000 miles) Removing the valve cover reveals what looks like a new motor(has 143k on it!) I have seen too many customers cars that have soo much sludge that I don't even think the oil flows any more! Stick to 3000 miles or 3 months and you will be OK!||||reg||1
